	Let $\hat{E}$ be the standard basis of $V = \F^n$ and let
	$F = \{v_1, \dots, v_m\} \subset V$ be a set of $m$ linearly independent
	vectors. Write $v_a = \sum_{b=1}^n A_{a,b}\, \hat{e}_b$, and, using a
	canonical Gaussian-elimination algorithm valid over arbitrary fields,
	transform the $m \times n$ matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form
	$B$. Let $J$ be the set of $m$ column indices containing the leading
	$1$ entry of each row of $B$. The \emph{canonical complement of $F$}
	is the set of $n - m$ standard basis vectors
	\[
		F^\perp = \{\hat{e}_b : b \notin J\}\;.
	\]
	The canonical complement is a \emph{set} of vectors, not a subspace.
	Although defined through the basis $F$, it depends only on
	$\mathrm{span}(F)$ and the ambient standard basis. Indeed, if $\pi_k$ is
	restriction to the first $k$ coordinates, the number of pivots among those
	coordinates is $\dim\pi_k(\mathrm{span}(F))$. Thus $b$ is a nonpivot
	coordinate precisely when
	\[
		\dim\pi_b(\mathrm{span}(F)) = \dim\pi_{b-1}(\mathrm{span}(F))\;,
	\]
	which is independent of the chosen basis and its order. The presentation
	through Gaussian elimination, adopted by \cite{Ji2020MIPStar}, makes the
	canonical complement efficiently computable. If $S$ is a register subspace
	spanned by $\hat{E}_0 \subseteq \hat{E}$, then the canonical complement of
	$\hat{E}_0$ is $\hat{E} \setminus \hat{E}_0$, and its span coincides with
	$S^\perp$.
\end{definition}

The row indices $a=1,\ldots,m$ and column indices $b=1,\ldots,n$ here and
in the following decomposition use the one-based numbering of
\cite{Ji2020MIPStar}. Their zero-based counterparts in Lean are $i=a-1$ and
$j=b-1$, respectively; the matrix entry $A_{a,b}$ corresponds to the entry
at $(i,j)$. If $p_a=p_{\mathrm{paper}}(a)$ is the pivot column of row $a$
and $p_{\mathrm{Lean}}$ is the zero-based pivot map, then
\[
  p_{\mathrm{paper}}(a)=p_{\mathrm{Lean}}(a-1)+1\;.
\]
The prefix lengths $b$ and $b-1$ therefore become $j+1$ and $j$; a prefix
length counts coordinates and is not itself a coordinate index. In particular,
$\pi_0$ restricts to the empty prefix. Similarly, a row sum
$\sum_{a=1}^m$ becomes the sum of the reindexed terms over
$i\in\{0,\ldots,m-1\}$, the finite index type $\mathrm{Fin}\,m$ in Lean.

